\end{align}
where we have implicitly used the fact that $h(\bm{U}\mathcal{O}) = h(\bm{U})$ for all orthogonal matrices $\mathcal{O}$.  If $2\ensuremath{\varepsilon}/\gamma < K$, then $\bm{U}_0$ is in the interior of $\mathbb{B}(\bm{\widehat U},K)$, and hence since $h$ is strongly convex within $\mathbb{B}(\bm{\widehat U},K)$, $\bm{U}_0$ is unique up to orthogonal transformation. 

Now suppose $\bm{\widetilde U} \in \mathbb{B}(\bm{\widehat U},K)$ is such that $h(\bm{\widetilde U}) > h(\bm{U}_0) + \ensuremath{\varepsilon}$.  
